# Elite AI Resume Builder & Job Tracker - Low Level Design (LLD)

This document outlines the architecture, tech stack, and module flows for the cross-platform application that parses CVs, scrapes global jobs, provides job market statistics, and generates ATS-friendly resumes.

## 1. Proposed Technology Stack (Highly Optimized)

To ensure the system is performant, scalable, and cost-effective using open-source tools, we have optimized the stack:

*   **Mobile Frontend (iOS & Android)**: **React Native (Expo)**
*   **Backend Framework**: **Python (FastAPI)**
*   **Primary Database**: **PostgreSQL** (with **pgvector** extension)
*   **Message Broker & Task Queue**: **RabbitMQ** (or Apache Kafka) + Celery
*   **Caching & Rate Limiting**: **Redis**
*   **AI/LLM Engine**: **Local OSS LLM (Llama 3 / Mistral via Ollama + vLLM)**
*   **Document Storage & Delivery**: **MinIO + Local File System**
*   **Web Scraping Engine**: **Playwright + Scrapy**

---

## 2. Optimized System Architecture

```mermaid
graph TD
  subgraph Client
    RN[React Native App]
    CDN[Content Delivery Network\n Cache Static PDFs]
  end

  subgraph API Gateway
    FastAPI[FastAPI Server\n Async WebSockets/REST]
  end

  subgraph Async Event Bus
    Rabbit[RabbitMQ / Kafka\n Message Broker]
  end

  subgraph Microservices & Workers
    UserSvc[User & Profile Service]
    ScraperWorker[Scrapy / Playwright\n Job Scraper Cluster]
    AIWorker[vLLM + Ollama\n AI Inference Engine]
    StatsAggregator[Stats Aggregation Worker]
  end

  subgraph Data Layer
    PG[(PostgreSQL\n Relational Data)]
    VectorDB[(ChromaDB / pgvector\n Semantic Embeddings)]
    Redis[(Redis\n High-Speed Cache)]
    MinIO[MinIO Object Storage\n Resumes & PDFs]
  end

  RN <-->|HTTPS| FastAPI
  RN -->|Download Resume| CDN
  CDN --> MinIO
  
  FastAPI --> UserSvc
  FastAPI --> Redis
  FastAPI --> Rabbit
  
  UserSvc --> PG
  UserSvc --> MinIO
  
  Rabbit --> ScraperWorker
  Rabbit --> AIWorker
  Rabbit --> StatsAggregator
  
  ScraperWorker -->|Raw Jobs| VectorDB
  ScraperWorker -->|Metrics| StatsAggregator
  
  StatsAggregator --> PG
  StatsAggregator -->|Update Cache| Redis
  
  AIWorker --> VectorDB
  AIWorker --> MinIO
```

---

## 3. Highly Performant Data Flows

### 3.1. Edge-Cached Resume Storage
*   Uploading a CV streams directly via FastAPI to **MinIO** in chunks, preventing RAM exhaustion on the server.
*   When a user downloads their generated ATS resume, it is served via a lightweight CDN (or NGINX reverse proxy caching) sitting in front of MinIO.

### 3.2. Vector/RAG AI Pipeline
*   **One-Time Process**: When the CV is uploaded, we summarize the skills and embed them into vectors stored in **ChromaDB/pgvector**.
*   **Fast Matching**: When a new Job Description is clicked, we instantly do a vector-math comparison to find which parts of the user's history match the JD, and *only send the matching context* to the LLM.

### 3.3. Event-Driven Web Scraping
*   The backend instantly queues the job in **RabbitMQ**.
*   A fleet of Python **Scrapy Workers** picks up the tasks in parallel. 
*   When finished, the worker populates the PostgreSQL database, updates the **Redis cache**, and sends a **WebSocket** notification back to the mobile app saying "Your market stats are ready!".

---

## 4. Hardware & Performance Evaluation

A critical distinction in this architecture is the **Client-Server Model**. The heavy lifting (AI, Web Scraping, Databases) will **NOT** run on the user's phone or tablet. Those components will run on a Centralized Backend Server (a self-hosted Windows/Linux PC or Cloud Server).

Here is the breakdown of hardware consumption:

### 4.1. The Client App (Phones & Tablets: Android / iOS)
The React Native app acts merely as a "Thin Client". It only renders the UI and makes HTTP requests to your Backend Server over the internet.
*   **CPU Consumption:** Very Low (Minimal processing, mostly UI rendering).
*   **Memory (RAM) Needed:** ~150MB - 300MB (Standard for modern mobile apps).
*   **Storage Space:** ~50MB for the application binary itself.
*   **Battery Drain:** Very Low (No heavy continuous processing; background execution is not required as pushing is handled by WebSockets).

### 4.2. The Backend Server Environment (Windows / Linux Desktop)
This is where the Open Source LLM (Ollama), Databases, and Scrapers physically run. You will host this on a solid desktop PC, laptop, or cloud server.
*   **CPU Requirements:** High. Minimum 8-Core CPU (e.g., AMD Ryzen 7 / Intel Core i7) for handling concurrent API traffic and Web Scraper headless browsers.
*   **Memory (RAM) Needed:** 
    *   **16GB RAM minimum** (If running smaller LLMs like Llama 3 8B quantized).
    *   **32GB+ RAM recommended** for faster inference + supporting the Vector DB + Scrapy workers simultaneously.
*   **Storage Space:** ~100GB Fast NVMe SSD.
    *   *Ollama Models:* ~5GB per model.
    *   *MinIO / PDFs:* Scalable based on users.
    *   *PostgreSQL/VectorDB:* ~10-20GB initial allocation.
*   **GPU (Optional but Highly Recommended):** An Nvidia RTX 3060/4060 (8GB-12GB VRAM) or better will exponentially speed up the AI Resume Generation compared to running entirely on the CPU.

### Summary
*   Users on **Android and iOS** will experience a lightning-fast, lightweight app that consumes virtually no resources.
*   The **Windows/Linux Server** that *you* run will do all the heavy lifting and requires a modern processor with at least 16GB of RAM to comfortably manage the Open Source AI and Web Scraper pipeline.
